<worksheet>
    <title>LT 6 Superball Patterns</title>
    <page>
    <description>In this activity, students will explore the patterns of a superball bouncing and analyze the height of each bounce.</description>
   
    <exercise>
        <introduction> In this you will be measuring the bounce height of a superball and recording the data to analyze the pattern of the bounces. </introduction> 
    
        <statement>
            <p>One person will drop a superball from designated heights, and another person will record the height of each bounce. Record your data in the table below.</p>
        
        <tabular top="minor" bottom="medium" left="major" right="major">
        <col>width=60% halign="center"</col>
        <col>width=70%</col>
        <row header="yes" halign="center" >
    <cell>Drop Height (inches)</cell>
    <cell>Bounce Height (inches)</cell>
  </row>
  <row halign="center">
    <cell>48</cell>
    <cell></cell>
  </row>
  <row halign="center">
    <cell>36</cell>
    <cell></cell>
  </row>      
<row halign="center">
    <cell>24</cell>
    <cell></cell>
  </row> 
  <row halign="center">
    <cell>12</cell>
    <cell></cell>
  </row>
  <row halign="center">
    <cell>6</cell>
    <cell></cell>
  </row>
</tabular>
</statement>

</exercise>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p> Let <m>D</m> represent the initial height and <m>B</m> represent the bounce height.  Use Desmos to ckreate a scattergram of the data.  Make a round sketch of the scattergram <m>(D,B)</m> of the data on the graph provided  Label the axes appropriately.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <figure>
                <image source="50x50 graph.png" width="40%">
                    <shortdescription>A distance and time graph with a constant increasing graph starting at (0,0), and a linear graph that increases, then is constant, and ends increasing.</shortdescription>
                </image>
            </figure>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="0.01in">
  <introduction>
    <p> 
      Use Linear Regression in Desmos to find the line of best fit for the data.  Write the equation of the line in function notation.
    </p>
  </introduction>
</exercise>
</page>
<page>
<exercise workspace="0.01in">
  <introduction>
    <p> 
      Use your model equation  to predict the bounce height of a superball dropped from a height of 60 inches.  Write a sentence to summarize your answer  Test your prediction. Show your work.
    </p>
  </introduction>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="0.01in">
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Use your model to predict from what height the ball needs to be dropped for it to bounce 30 inches.  Write a sentence to summarize your answer.  Test your prediction!
    </p>
  </introduction>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="0.01in">
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Find B(10) and summarize your solution with a sentence that uses the context of this problem.
    </p>
  </introduction>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="0.01in">
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Find D so that B(d) = 20 and summarize your solution with a sentence that uses the context of this problem.
    </p>
  </introduction>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="0.01in">
  <introduction>
    <p>
      What is something that you learned from this lesson, or maybe learned better?
    </p>
  </introduction>
</exercise>
</page>
</worksheet>
